      "scope": "Grade 6–8; Modular section (100–400 words)",
      "blueprint": true,
      "blueprintProse": "The PAS framework distributes copy to surface and agitate operational bottlenecks. The Problem component accounts for 25% of the total word budget, defining an immediate, recognizable operational friction in the customer's native vernacular. The Agitate phase is the structural anchor, demanding 45% of the copy to expose the compounding, systemic consequences of leaving the problem unresolved. The Solve phase concludes the unit at 30% of the word count, presenting the product as the single logical mechanism capable of defusing the agitated friction. \nPAS is driven by prospect theory and loss aversion. Behavioral economics demonstrates that the disutility of an unaddressed loss is perceived as roughly twice as motivating as the utility of an equivalent gain. Agitation elevates cognitive dissonance and emotional arousal, preventing the prospect from rationalizing delay and priming them to embrace the presented solution.",
